[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Milestone two's semantics sit in the tool, which is the wrong layer

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[EVIDENCE]
UID: MECH-MILESTONE-TWO-SEMANTICS-MISPLACED
TITLE: Milestone two's semantics sit in the tool, which is the wrong layer
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Milestone two shipped a writer for the graph, and with it a body of SEMANTIC
logic that DEC-LAYER-STACK places in L3: fingerprinting, contract hashing,
readiness and the rules around signing. That logic lives in the tool's own
scripts rather than in a semantic definition that decomposes onto grammar and
hooks.

THIS NODE MAKES THE CLASSIFICATION ONLY, and deliberately makes no judgement
about the logic itself. The placement is wrong; whether the logic is right is a
separate question that stays open. The logic may well be adopted wholesale when
L3 is defined, or it may be replaced -- neither outcome changes the placement
finding, and neither should be decided by whether the code happens to work.

What is affected, named so the eventual judgement has a list rather than a
search: dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py, dev/scripts/fp-check.py, dev/scripts/fp-accept.py,
docs/sdoc/status.py's readiness walk, and the parts of dev/scripts/sdoc_model.py
and sdoc_cli.py that know what a fingerprint means. The grammar-derived option
surface is NOT in scope: deriving flags from the grammar is L1 consuming L0,
which is the layering working.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recording the placement now, separately from any judgement of the logic, is what
keeps the two from being decided together. If the finding waits until L3 is
designed, the argument becomes "the code works, why move it", which is an answer
to a question nobody asked -- correct logic in the wrong layer is still the
wrong layer.

It also protects the logic. Classified now and judged later, good rules can be
lifted into L3 on their merits. Left unclassified, they become the de facto
semantic layer by accretion, and nothing ever decides they should be.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Classification made 2026-08-28 by the operator. Judgement on the logic is
explicitly PENDING and is not this node's to make. Nothing is being reverted,
and milestone two's DEPTH is not lowered by this: it shipped what it was asked
for, and the layering rule it crossed was not written down until afterwards.

FOUR REFUSALS THE MILESTONE'S OWN CLOSING SUMMARY OFFERED AS FEATURES, and they
do not classify together. Two are the layering working and two are the leak, so
the summary reads as one achievement where there are two different things.

DERIVED, and therefore correct. A wrong prefix ("SLICE-WRONG is not a MECHANISM
name: the grammar gives MECHANISM the prefix MECH-") and an undeclared role
("--role Crosses: invalid choice") are both read straight off the grammar. That
is L1 consuming L0, which is the shape the layering asks for. If the grammar
changes, both refusals change with it and nobody edits the tool.

BAKED IN, and therefore the leak. The authorship write guard ("--authored-by is
not a flag of this tool ... there is no override") encodes WHO MAY WRITE A
FIELD, which is a rule about authority and belongs in L3. The tool enforces it
by not having a flag, so the rule cannot be read, cited, overridden or changed
without editing the tool. The File path rule ("has a './' segment") is the same
shape and smaller: a convention about form that no grammar declares.

The tell that separates them: ask whether the grammar could change the answer.
If yes it is derived; if the answer is fixed in the script, it is semantics
wearing a validator's clothes.

THE GUARD BIT THIS SESSION, which is worth recording as a positive control
rather than a complaint. Two of the decisions written on 2026-08-28 carry rules
the operator authored, and neither could be marked as theirs, because the tool
has no flag for it. The refusal is behaving exactly as designed and the design
is in the wrong layer -- the correct fix is not an override flag, it is that
authorship authority is an L3 rule the tool consumes rather than one it holds.

Retyped from MECHANISM to EVIDENCE on 2026-08-30 by the mechanism mood test under DEC-NODE-FAMILIES, with medium confidence after a second opinion: it records what was observed once, not how something always behaves. Noted for a later split: NOTES carry a universal heuristic ('ask whether the grammar could change the answer: if yes it is derived; if fixed in the script it is semantics') and a rule sentence ('authorship authority is an L3 rule the tool consumes rather than one it holds'). The UID keeps its prefix: DEC-UID-OUTLIVES-TYPE.
